1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,664
On a spring morning in 1526,

2
00:00:02,664 --> 00:00:06,035
twelve thousand men from Central Asia stood

3
00:00:06,035 --> 00:00:08,548
in front of an army many times

4
00:00:08,548 --> 00:00:11,589
their size, with around a thousand war

5
00:00:11,589 --> 00:00:12,382
elephants.

6
00:00:12,852 --> 00:00:14,975
By noon, the Sultan of Delhi was

7
00:00:14,975 --> 00:00:17,046
dead, and a new empire had been

8
00:00:17,046 --> 00:00:17,417
born.

9
00:00:17,637 --> 00:00:20,014
It would rule India for three hundred

10
00:00:20,014 --> 00:00:21,968
years and build the Taj Mahal.

11
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:24,640
Its founder was a refugee prince who

12
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:26,924
had lost his homeland three times.

13
00:00:27,394 --> 00:00:29,570
This is the First Battle of Panipat.

14
00:00:29,790 --> 00:00:32,197
If you enjoy history told with real

15
00:00:32,197 --> 00:00:34,716
sources, tap like, it helps more than

16
00:00:34,716 --> 00:00:35,443
you think.

17
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:38,537
His name was Zahir al-Din Muhammad,

18
00:00:38,537 --> 00:00:40,662
but history calls him Babur,

19
00:00:40,662 --> 00:00:42,036
which means tiger.

20
00:00:42,256 --> 00:00:45,702
He was born in 1483 in Andijan,

21
00:00:45,702 --> 00:00:48,087
in the Fergana Valley,

22
00:00:48,087 --> 00:00:50,384
in today's Uzbekistan.

23
00:00:50,854 --> 00:00:53,327
On his father's side he descended from

24
00:00:53,327 --> 00:00:56,230
Timur, the conqueror the West calls Tamerlane.

25
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:57,894
On his mother's side,

26
00:00:57,894 --> 00:00:59,117
from Genghis Khan.

27
00:00:59,587 --> 00:01:02,296
At eleven he became ruler of Fergana.

28
00:01:02,516 --> 00:01:05,030
He captured Samarkand, the city of his

29
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:06,505
ancestor, and lost it.

30
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:07,974
He took it again,

31
00:01:07,974 --> 00:01:09,279
and lost it again,

32
00:01:09,279 --> 00:01:11,946
driven out by the Uzbek leader Muhammad

33
00:01:11,946 --> 00:01:12,571
Shaybani.

34
00:01:13,041 --> 00:01:16,013
By 1504 Babur had given up on

35
00:01:16,013 --> 00:01:19,547
his homeland and taken Kabul instead.

36
00:01:19,767 --> 00:01:22,093
From Kabul, he started looking south.

37
00:01:22,563 --> 00:01:23,715
Look at the map.

38
00:01:23,935 --> 00:01:26,350
South of the Khyber Pass lay the

39
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:29,309
Delhi Sultanate, ruled by Ibrahim Lodi of

40
00:01:29,309 --> 00:01:31,060
the Afghan Lodi dynasty.

41
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:33,798
It covered Punjab and the rich Ganges

42
00:01:33,798 --> 00:01:34,245
plain.

43
00:01:34,715 --> 00:01:37,218
But Ibrahim had made enemies among his

44
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:37,979
own nobles.

45
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:40,322
One of them, Daulat Khan Lodi,

46
00:01:40,322 --> 00:01:41,928
the governor of Punjab,

47
00:01:41,928 --> 00:01:44,279
offered to switch to Babur's side.

48
00:01:44,499 --> 00:01:46,782
For Babur, it was an open door.

49
00:01:47,252 --> 00:01:49,873
Babur crossed the Indus with about twelve

50
00:01:49,873 --> 00:01:50,729
thousand men.

51
00:01:50,949 --> 00:01:53,438
That number comes from Babur himself,

52
00:01:53,438 --> 00:01:54,512
in his memoirs.

53
00:01:54,982 --> 00:01:57,551
Ibrahim marched north from Delhi to meet

54
00:01:57,551 --> 00:01:59,907
him, and the two armies stopped near

55
00:01:59,907 --> 00:02:01,245
the town of Panipat,

56
00:02:01,245 --> 00:02:03,707
about ninety kilometres north of Delhi.

57
00:02:03,927 --> 00:02:04,973
For about a week,

58
00:02:04,973 --> 00:02:06,637
they just watched each other.

59
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:09,770
Babur was told Ibrahim had one hundred

60
00:02:09,770 --> 00:02:12,376
thousand men and a thousand elephants.

61
00:02:12,596 --> 00:02:15,028
Modern historians are more careful.

62
00:02:15,248 --> 00:02:18,123
One estimate is fifty to seventy thousand

63
00:02:18,123 --> 00:02:18,768
soldiers.

64
00:02:18,988 --> 00:02:21,051
Some sources go as low as forty

65
00:02:21,051 --> 00:02:21,633
thousand.

66
00:02:22,103 --> 00:02:23,833
Whatever the real number,

67
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:25,736
Babur was badly outnumbered,

68
00:02:25,736 --> 00:02:27,120
maybe three to one,

69
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:27,927
maybe more.

70
00:02:28,147 --> 00:02:30,557
And remember, a general always has a

71
00:02:30,557 --> 00:02:33,075
reason to make his enemy sound bigger.

72
00:02:33,545 --> 00:02:35,060
So Babur dug in.

73
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,968
He tied around seven hundred carts together

74
00:02:37,968 --> 00:02:40,129
with ropes, in what he called the

75
00:02:40,129 --> 00:02:42,870
Ottoman fashion, the same method the Ottoman

76
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:45,136
Turks had used against the Persians.

77
00:02:45,356 --> 00:02:47,602
Between the carts he left gaps for

78
00:02:47,602 --> 00:02:49,047
cavalry to charge out.

79
00:02:49,517 --> 00:02:51,752
He protected one flank with the town

80
00:02:51,752 --> 00:02:54,039
of Panipat and the other with ditches

81
00:02:54,039 --> 00:02:55,106
and cut branches.

82
00:02:55,576 --> 00:02:57,825
And he had something few armies in

83
00:02:57,825 --> 00:02:59,913
India had used like this before:

84
00:02:59,913 --> 00:03:01,037
gunpowder weapons.

85
00:03:01,257 --> 00:03:02,921
How many is disputed.

86
00:03:03,141 --> 00:03:05,720
One modern study says fifteen to twenty

87
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:06,213
cannon.

88
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:09,059
Another reading of the sources says only

89
00:03:09,059 --> 00:03:11,575
two small cannon are clearly mentioned.

90
00:03:12,045 --> 00:03:13,849
What is clear is that some of

91
00:03:13,849 --> 00:03:15,799
his men carried matchlock muskets.

92
00:03:16,019 --> 00:03:18,081
Which do you think won this battle:

93
00:03:18,081 --> 00:03:19,375
the guns, the archers,

94
00:03:19,375 --> 00:03:20,286
or the tactics?

95
00:03:20,506 --> 00:03:21,957
Tell me in the comments.

96
00:03:22,427 --> 00:03:24,517
On the morning of the 21st of

97
00:03:24,517 --> 00:03:26,608
April, Ibrahim finally attacked.

98
00:03:26,828 --> 00:03:29,254
His army pushed toward Babur's line of

99
00:03:29,254 --> 00:03:31,364
carts and got jammed in front of

100
00:03:31,364 --> 00:03:31,628
it.

101
00:03:32,098 --> 00:03:34,785
Then Babur sent his cavalry around both

102
00:03:34,785 --> 00:03:36,786
flanks, an old steppe tactic,

103
00:03:36,786 --> 00:03:39,130
to hit the Sultan's army from the

104
00:03:39,130 --> 00:03:40,674
sides and from behind.

105
00:03:41,144 --> 00:03:43,831
Mounted archers poured arrows into the packed

106
00:03:43,831 --> 00:03:44,643
mass of men.

107
00:03:44,863 --> 00:03:46,217
The guns added noise,

108
00:03:46,217 --> 00:03:48,873
smoke and fear, and the elephants panicked.

109
00:03:49,093 --> 00:03:50,843
By about noon it was over.

110
00:03:51,313 --> 00:03:53,710
Ibrahim Lodi died on the battlefield,

111
00:03:53,710 --> 00:03:55,345
with his men around him.

112
00:03:55,565 --> 00:03:57,207
How many died with him?

113
00:03:57,427 --> 00:04:01,139
Babur's men estimated fifteen to sixteen thousand.

114
00:04:01,359 --> 00:04:03,684
The people of Agra said forty to

115
00:04:03,684 --> 00:04:04,730
fifty thousand.

116
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:07,603
One modern historian puts it at twenty

117
00:04:07,603 --> 00:04:10,267
thousand, with thousands more killed in the

118
00:04:10,267 --> 00:04:10,789
retreat.

119
00:04:11,009 --> 00:04:14,551
Babur's own losses were not reliably recorded.

120
00:04:14,771 --> 00:04:16,800
Once again, the sources disagree,

121
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:18,205
and we should say so.

122
00:04:18,675 --> 00:04:21,453
Within days Babur's men took Delhi and

123
00:04:21,453 --> 00:04:21,875
Agra.

124
00:04:22,095 --> 00:04:26,095
The Delhi Sultanate, which had existed since

125
00:04:26,095 --> 00:04:27,557
1206, was gone.

126
00:04:27,777 --> 00:04:29,717
In its place stood what we now

127
00:04:29,717 --> 00:04:31,147
call the Mughal Empire.

128
00:04:31,617 --> 00:04:33,555
A year later, at Khanwa,

129
00:04:33,555 --> 00:04:36,786
Babur defeated a coalition of Rajput kings

130
00:04:36,786 --> 00:04:38,272
led by Rana Sanga,

131
00:04:38,272 --> 00:04:40,727
and his new kingdom was secure.

132
00:04:41,197 --> 00:04:43,664
A popular story says that at Agra

133
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:46,071
his son Humayun was given a huge

134
00:04:46,071 --> 00:04:49,260
diamond, often identified with the Koh-i-Noor,

135
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:51,607
now in the British Crown Jewels.

136
00:04:51,827 --> 00:04:54,605
Historians still argue about whether it is

137
00:04:54,605 --> 00:04:56,882
the same stone, so treat that one

138
00:04:56,882 --> 00:04:57,715
as a maybe.

139
00:04:58,185 --> 00:05:00,425
Here's the part most people don't know.

140
00:05:00,645 --> 00:05:03,973
Babur wrote one of the first autobiographies

141
00:05:03,973 --> 00:05:06,533
in Islamic history, the Baburnama,

142
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:08,645
in his own Turkic language.

143
00:05:08,865 --> 00:05:10,636
And he didn't hide his feelings.

144
00:05:11,106 --> 00:05:13,793
He complained that India had no good

145
00:05:13,793 --> 00:05:16,235
horses, no good grapes or melons,

146
00:05:16,235 --> 00:05:18,189
no cold water and no ice.

147
00:05:18,409 --> 00:05:20,941
He planted gardens to remind him of

148
00:05:20,941 --> 00:05:21,353
home.

149
00:05:21,823 --> 00:05:23,999
He ruled for only four years.

150
00:05:24,219 --> 00:05:28,208
Babur died in Agra in 1530.

151
00:05:28,428 --> 00:05:30,322
As he wished, he was buried in

152
00:05:30,322 --> 00:05:31,469
a garden in Kabul,

153
00:05:31,469 --> 00:05:33,164
where his tomb still stands.

154
00:05:33,634 --> 00:05:38,227
In 1526, Babur's kingdom stretched from Kabul

155
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:39,181
to Agra.

156
00:05:39,401 --> 00:05:43,415
At its height, under his great-great-great-grandson Aurangzeb,

157
00:05:43,415 --> 00:05:45,996
the Mughal Empire covered most of the

158
00:05:45,996 --> 00:05:47,315
Indian subcontinent.

159
00:05:47,785 --> 00:05:49,931
It lasted until 1857,

160
00:05:49,931 --> 00:05:54,057
when the British removed the last emperor.

161
00:05:54,277 --> 00:05:56,488
Today, those lands are India,

162
00:05:56,488 --> 00:05:59,141
Pakistan, Afghanistan and Bangladesh.

163
00:05:59,361 --> 00:06:01,595
And the boy from Andijan has a

164
00:06:01,595 --> 00:06:03,769
statue and a park in his home

165
00:06:03,769 --> 00:06:05,121
city in Uzbekistan.

166
00:06:05,591 --> 00:06:07,269
So what really won Panipat:

167
00:06:07,269 --> 00:06:08,541
the guns, the carts,

168
00:06:08,541 --> 00:06:09,965
or the steppe horsemen?

169
00:06:10,185 --> 00:06:11,780
Tell me what you think in the

170
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:12,254
comments.

171
00:06:12,474 --> 00:06:14,965
And subscribe, because next we go to

172
00:06:14,965 --> 00:06:17,569
another field where a small army faced

173
00:06:17,569 --> 00:06:18,191
a giant.

174
00:06:18,411 --> 00:06:19,777
You won't want to miss it.

